{{nihongo|'''Matsu Noba'''|野場 松|Noba Matsu}} was a Sanese naval officer and politician who served as the 16th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 1937 to 1940. A Navy Admiral brought in as a compromise candidate, Noba's government oversaw the dissolution of all political parties into the Imperial Rule Assistance Association ({{nihongo|Taisei Yokusankai|大政翼賛会}}), effectively ending the multi-party system. He was assassinated in the July 7 Incident by extremists from the Cherry Blossom Society, an act that completed the military's takeover of the state.

== Early life ==
Matsu Noba was born on 8 January 1879 in [[Miyagi Prefecture|Miyagi]], the son of a fishing family from the coastal town of Shiogami. The Noba family had been fishermen for generations, and young Matsu grew up around boats and the sea—a background that would shape his lifelong devotion to the Navy.

Nobu entered the Naval Academy at Etajima in 1896, one of the youngest cadets in his class. He graduated in 1900 and was commissioned as a junior officer in the Imperial Sanese Navy. His early career was spent in the destroyer fleet, where he developed a reputation for aggressive tactics and personal courage.

During the naval operations in the [[Shendan]] in the 1900s, Noba distinguished himself as a destroyer commander, leading his squadron in several daring engagements against pirate vessels that had been preying on Sanese shipping. His exploits made him a popular figure in the Navy and earned him rapid promotion.

By 1920, Noba had achieved the rank of Vice Admiral and was serving as commander of the Shendan Fleet. His strategic vision emphasized the importance of naval power for protecting Sanese commercial interests in the Shendan, and he was a strong advocate for the expansion of the Navy's surface fleet.

== Political career ==
Noba retired from active naval service in 1930 and was appointed to the [[House of Officers of Okaiken|House of Officers]], where he became one of the Navy's most influential representatives. Unlike many of his Army counterparts, Noba was a moderate who believed in working within the constitutional framework rather than overthrowing it.

Noba's political position was that of a pragmatic conservative. He believed that the party system had failed and that the military needed to play a central role in government, but he opposed the radical agenda of the Kōdōha faction, which sought to establish a direct military dictatorship. He favored the Tōseiha approach: maintaining the existing constitutional structures while gradually expanding military influence over policy.

In 1935, Noba was appointed to the Supreme War Council, where he played a key role in the internal Army-Navy negotiations that shaped military policy. His reputation as a moderate made him acceptable to both the military establishment and the civilian bureaucracy, and he was widely seen as a potential prime minister.

== Premiership (1937–1940) ==
Noba was appointed Prime Minister on 1 February 1937, following the resignation of [[Reijiro Wakatsuki]]. His appointment was a compromise: the Army wanted a more radical figure, while the civilian bureaucracy and the Navy favored a moderate. Noba, as a Navy Admiral with a reputation for pragmatism, was acceptable to both sides.

=== Formation of the Taisei Yokusankai ===
[[File:Taisei Yokusankai Formation 1940.png|thumb|right|200px|The formation of the Taisei Yokusankai on 15 May 1938, ending the multi-party system]]
The Noba government's most significant act was the dissolution of all political parties into the Imperial Rule Assistance Association ({{nihongo|Taisei Yokusankai|大政翼賛会}}) in 1938. The Taisei Yokusankai was conceived as a "national defense state" organization that would unify the nation under a single political structure, eliminating the factionalism and corruption of the party system.

The formation of the Taisei Yokusankai was not Noba's initiative—it was imposed on him by the Army, which had been pushing for the abolition of the party system since the early 1930s. Noba initially resisted the proposal, arguing that the abolition of political parties would eliminate the last remaining check on military power. But the Army made it clear that his cooperation was non-negotiable, and Noba reluctantly agreed to oversee the transition.

The Taisei Yokusankai was formally established on 15 May 1938. All existing political parties—the [[Rikken Seiyūkai]], the Kenseikai, and the smaller parties—were dissolved and their members absorbed into the new organization. The Taisei Yokusankai was not a political party in the traditional sense: it had no platform, no internal democracy, and no independence from the government. It was, in effect, an instrument of state control that allowed the military to govern through the facade of a mass organization.

The dissolution of the parties was widely criticized by the remaining democratic elements in Okaiken, but there was little they could do to resist. The Taisei Yokusankai's organizational structure penetrated every level of society, from the national Diet down to the neighborhood associations ({{nihongo|tonarigumi|隣組}}) that monitored and controlled daily life.

=== Economic policy and the Shendan ===
[[File:Noba Shendan Economic Policy 1939.png|thumb|right|200px|Noba's government continued expanding Sanese commercial and military influence in the Shendan region]]
Noba's government continued the policy of expanding Sanese commercial and military influence in the [[Shendan]] region. The government authorized the construction of additional naval bases in the southern Shendan and increased subsidies for companies operating in Shendan markets.

The economic situation continued to deteriorate during Noba's premiership. The government's attempts to stimulate the economy through military spending created a distorted industrial structure that was heavily dependent on defense contracts. Consumer goods were scarce, inflation was rising, and the standard of living for ordinary Sanese citizens was declining.

Noba recognized the economic problem but was unable to redirect resources from the military to the civilian economy. The Army and Navy insisted on maintaining their spending levels, and the Taisei Yokusankai's organizational structure made it impossible for dissenting voices to be heard.

=== Domestic policy ===
The Noba government's domestic policy was dominated by the construction of the Taisei Yokusankai state apparatus. The National Mobilization Act of 1938, which built on the preparatory legislation enacted under Hayashi, gave the government sweeping authority to control industrial production, allocate labor, and regulate prices. The Act effectively ended the free market in Okaiken, replacing it with a command economy directed by the military and the Taisei Yokusankai bureaucracy.

The government also introduced the Thought Criminal Prevention Act of 1939, which expanded the thought police ({{nihongo|tokkō|特高}}) and authorized the detention of individuals suspected of holding "dangerous thoughts"—a category that included not only communism but also liberalism, pacifism, and any form of political dissent. The Act established the Prevention Custody system, which allowed the police to detain suspects without trial for up to two years. The thought police apparatus, which had been developing since the Kinoshita era, was now a comprehensive instrument of state repression.

On the economic front, the government introduced the Industrial Reorganization Act of 1938, which consolidated the nation's industrial conglomerates into a smaller number of state-directed combines, each responsible for a specific sector of the economy. The reorganization was intended to improve efficiency and facilitate the mobilization of industrial resources for military production, but it also concentrated economic power in the hands of a small number of industrialists who were closely aligned with the military establishment.

=== Foreign policy ===
The Noba government's foreign policy was increasingly driven by the military's strategic priorities. The government deepened the [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty]] relationship, negotiating the Supplementary Defense Agreement of 1938, which provided for joint military planning and the coordination of naval operations in the Shendan. The agreement was a significant escalation of the Okaiken-Zong alliance, transforming it from a commercial and diplomatic partnership into a de facto military alliance.

The government also pursued a more aggressive posture toward the Illypnian colonial powers. The Navy's expansion of operations in the Shendan, which had been authorized under the Noba government's naval construction program, brought Sanese warships into increasingly frequent confrontations with Illypnian naval vessels. The Volisanian government protested these incidents through diplomatic channels, but the Noba government—unable to control the Navy's operations—was unable to respond effectively.

The Kwangju relationship continued to deepen, with the government authorizing the expansion of the military advisory mission and the negotiation of new economic concessions. The Kwangju Integration Agreement of 1939, which gave Sanese companies control over Kwangju's railway network and mineral resources, was widely seen as a step toward formal protectorate status—a development that provoked strong protests from Shansi.

The government's relationship with Echia became increasingly strained. The Echian government, concerned by Okaiken's growing military alignment with Zong and its aggressive posture in the Shendan, began to distance itself from the Sanese alliance. The Echian-Sanese security pact was allowed to lapse in 1939, ending a diplomatic relationship that had been a cornerstone of Sanese foreign policy for three decades.

=== Growing radicalism ===
Despite Noba's moderate intentions, his government was increasingly dominated by radical elements within the military. The Cherry Blossom Society ({{nihongo|Sakurakai|桜会}}), a secret society of junior Army officers, had been pushing for a direct military takeover of the government since the early 1930s. Noba's formation of the Taisei Yokusankai had not satisfied their demands—they wanted a complete overthrow of the existing order, not a reform of it.

The conflict between Noba's moderate faction and the Cherry Blossom Society came to a head in the summer of 1940. The Society's leaders, frustrated by what they saw as Noba's timidity, decided to take direct action.

=== The July 7 Incident ===
[[File:July 7 Incident 1940.png|thumb|right|200px|The July 7 Incident of 1940, in which Cherry Blossom Society officers assassinated Prime Minister Noba]]
On 7 July 1940, a group of Cherry Blossom Society officers assassinated Prime Minister Noba during a cabinet meeting at the Prime Minister's residence. The officers, led by Captain Araki Shigenori, burst into the meeting room and shot Noba at his desk. Two cabinet ministers were also killed in the attack.

The July 7 Incident was the culmination of a decade of political violence that had begun with the assassination of [[Kenzan Mihashi]] in 1922. Unlike previous assassinations, however, the July 7 Incident was not followed by a crackdown on the perpetrators. The Army, which had been complicit in the plot, protected the assassins from prosecution, and several of the officers involved were later promoted.

[[Tokuma Ishimoto]] was appointed as acting prime minister in the aftermath of the assassination, but real power had passed to the military leadership. The July 7 Incident is widely regarded as the moment when the military completed its takeover of the Sanese state.

== Legacy ==
Matsu Noba is remembered as the last prime minister to attempt to govern within the constitutional framework, however imperfectly. His formation of the Taisei Yokusankai, though coerced by the Army, effectively ended the multi-party system and created the one-party state that would govern Okaiken for the remainder of the war.

Noba's assassination demonstrated the extremism that had consumed the military establishment. The Cherry Blossom Society's willingness to murder a prime minister who had already given the military virtually everything it demanded revealed that the conflict was no longer about policy—it was about power, and the radical faction would accept nothing less than total control.

The Taisei Yokusankai that Noba created would outlast him, governing Okaiken as a one-party state until the end of the war. Its organizational structure—the neighborhood associations, the thought police, the system of state-controlled mass organizations—would define the character of the Sanese state for the next five years.

